  "cards": [
   [
    "Warm water is a starting condition",
    "Tropical oceans supply heat and moisture. Converging trade-wind flows and tropical disturbances can organise rising moist air. Formation usually needs water around26.5°C or warmer through a substantial depth, moist air and low vertical wind shear. Warm water alone does not guarantee a cyclone."
   ],
   [
    "A reinforcing circulation",
    "Air rises and water vapour condenses, releasing latent heat. A warm core and falling surface pressure support further inflow. Earth’s rotation helps organise the circulation away from the equator: surface air spirals anticlockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern. Outflow occurs high above the storm."
   ],
   [
    "Eye, eyewall and weakening",
    "In a mature cyclone, strong rising air and winds surround a relatively calm eye where air sinks. Rainbands extend outward. Crossing land, cooler water or encountering strong wind shear can weaken the storm; dangerous rain and flooding can continue. The eye passing overhead is not proof that the storm has finished."
   ]
  ],
